Use Next.js + TypeScript.

Normalization logic must remain plain TypeScript and independent of UI.

Pipeline:

raw row
→ source-specific extraction
→ common normalization
→ validation
→ metrics
→ JSON
→ dashboard

Rules:
- preserve raw title/source identifiers
- source adapters should produce one common NormalizedProduct shape
- common unit conversion should be reused across sources
- distinguish:
  - package count
  - purchasable quantity
  - physical dimensions
  - product specifications
  - strength/dosage
- derive unit price only when denominator semantics are trustworthy
- ambiguous cases should produce warnings rather than guessed values
- keep processing deterministic and testable
- avoid premature generic frameworks or category-rule engines
- keep dashboard presentation separate from normalization

## Code readability and learning-oriented structure

The codebase should be easy to navigate and understand during live review.

- Every function should have a short one-line JSDoc/comment stating its purpose.
- Keep functions small and single-purpose with descriptive names.
- Add short inline comments around important parsing/normalization/validation branches explaining **why the rule is safe or why a value is rejected**.
- Prefer comments such as:
  `// Dimensions describe product identity here, not a pricing quantity.`
  over comments that merely restate syntax.
- In source adapters, keep the flow visually clear:
  identity → taxonomy → package/quantity → specifications → pricing → quality.
- Use named helper functions instead of dense regex/condition blocks inside adapters.
- Keep regex definitions named and close to the parser that owns their semantics.
- Avoid excessive comments on trivial assignments; comments should provide a quick mental map and explain business meaning.